---

`oriel wrap <url>` creates an editable Oriel project that opens a website in
a desktop window. `oriel pake` is an alias for the same command. Available
since Oriel 0.9.4.

The app uses the system WebView: WebKitGTK on Linux, WebView2 on Windows,
and WKWebView on macOS. It loads the live website, so it needs the same
network access and account as the browser version.

# Create and run

First [install the CLI and platform dependencies]($link.page('docs/getting-started'))
and run `oriel doctor --fix`. Wrapped apps need no Node.js or npm: their
generated frontend is a static loading page.

```sh
oriel wrap https://web.whatsapp.com --name whatsapp --title "WhatsApp"
cd whatsapp
oriel run
```

The command creates `./whatsapp` and fetches its Zig dependencies. Without
`--name`, it derives a name from the URL's hostname. Use a new or empty
directory; it refuses to overwrite a non-empty one.

To launch immediately after creating the project:

```sh
oriel wrap https://example.com --name example --run
```

# Tray and window behavior

The tray is **enabled by default**. Its menu has **Show / Hide** and **Quit**.
Closing the window hides it while the app keeps running; choose Quit to exit.

Use `--no-tray` for an app that exits when its window closes:

```sh
oriel wrap https://example.com --name example --no-tray
```

On Linux, your desktop needs system tray support to show the icon.

# Icon and browser identity

Pass `--icon ./icon.png` to use a local PNG. Otherwise Oriel tries Google's
favicon service, then DuckDuckGo, and falls back to its own icon if no PNG
is available. If the supplied icon path does not exist, it also tries to
save the fetched favicon there. The project stores its icon in `assets/icon.png`.

Some sites require a particular browser identity. On **Linux**, use
`--user-agent chrome`, `safari`, `firefox`, or a quoted custom string.
WhatsApp URLs automatically select the Chrome preset when this option is
omitted. Windows and macOS currently retain the WebView's default user agent.
Changing the user agent does not change the WebView engine or its features.

# Package on Linux

Create a ReleaseSafe AppImage in one command:

```sh
oriel wrap https://web.whatsapp.com \
  --name whatsapp \
  --title "WhatsApp" \
  --id com.whatsapp.desktop \
  --tray \
  --user-agent chrome \
  --package
```

The output is in `whatsapp/zig-out/package/`. `--package` currently invokes
the Linux `package-appimage` build step. The AppImage relies on the host's
GTK 4 and WebKitGTK 6.0 runtime libraries; it does not bundle them.

For an existing project:

```sh
cd whatsapp
oriel build
oriel package
```

The generated `build.zig` selects `.appimage` as its package format.
For Windows or macOS distribution, edit that packaging configuration for
the target platform and follow [Building & packaging]($link.page('docs/building'))
and [Code signing]($link.page('docs/signing')).

# Options

| Option | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `<url>` | Website to open; `https://` is added if omitted |
| `--name <name>` | Project directory and executable name; start with a letter, use letters, digits, `-` and `_`, at most 32 characters |
| `--title <title>` | Window title and package display name; derived from the name by default |
| `--id <app-id>` | Application identifier in reverse-DNS form |
| `--icon <path>` | Local PNG, or a destination for a fetched favicon if missing |
| `--tray` / `--no-tray` | Enable (default) or disable tray and close-to-tray behavior |
| `--user-agent <ua>` | Linux: `chrome`, `safari`, `firefox`, or a custom string |
| `--devtools` | Enable developer tools and forward console messages to stdout |
| `--package` | Build a ReleaseSafe Linux AppImage after creating the project |
| `--run` | Build and run after creating the project |
| `--oriel-ref <ref>` | Oriel Git tag or commit to depend on; defaults to the CLI's bundled reference |
| `--oriel-path <dir>` | Use a local Oriel checkout |

Run `oriel wrap --help` for the installed CLI's options.

# Customize the generated app

The project is ordinary Zig source that you can edit and rebuild:

| File | What to change |
|---|---|
| `src/main.zig` | URL, window size, tray menu, user agent, navigation and capabilities |
| `build.zig` | Package metadata, formats, enabled modules and OS permission descriptions |
| `build.zig.zon` | Oriel dependency |
| `assets/icon.png` | App icon |
| `frontend/index.html` | Embedded loading page |

The generated app declares notification and microphone permissions and
configures allowed origins and capability rules using HTTPS hostnames.
For most sites, its wildcard rule uses the last two hostname labels;
WhatsApp gets dedicated `.whatsapp.com` and `.whatsapp.net` rules.
Review these rules in `src/main.zig` for HTTP URLs, custom ports, domains
such as `example.co.uk`, or additional login and service domains.
Other links open in the default browser.
The operating system and WebView still decide whether to grant access.
See [Permissions]($link.page('docs/permissions')) for the security model.
